  "behavior_evidence": [
    "Sixteen native rows cover head/middle/tail single erase, overlapping and disjoint range shifts, tail/full erasure, six rejected index/range cases, and three throwing move-assignment phases.",
    "Every callback and destructor records its actual original vector slot index. Remaining payload ownership is checked against original boxed/unique_ptr resource addresses; container allocation and capacity remain stable.",
    "Native and Rust assignment throws before the first completed shift and after earlier shifts retain the original length, completed destination/source mutations and exact resource identities. No tail destruction runs when assignment fails.",
    "Two original nontrivial AsciiString heap-buffer erasure declarations now invoke AsciiString::move_assign through the actual callback adapters; all original inputs/assertions remain.",
    "Implementation uses disjoint split_at_mut slices; no unsafe code or Clone/Default item bound was added. Borrowed values and maximum invalid indices have separate regression checks.",
    "Baseline compiler rejects both missing erasure APIs; candidate compiles/runs them. LinearVector covariance, Send/Sync/Unpin and unwind traits remain unchanged."
  ],
  "remaining_callable_work": [
    "LinearVector Insert still lacks explicit native shifted-slot move-construction/destruction callbacks. This remains the next concrete slice.",
    "The existing erase/erase_range convenience methods retain Rust ownership-shift policy and are now documented as such; native element assignment uses explicit callbacks.",
    "Queued move candidate ece5dc2155bb0f8ce6875003a544c5e94804198d is independent of this branch. It was intentionally not copied or modified while main was frozen."
  ],
  "limitations": [
    "The common native domain uses callbacks that preserve valid live destination/source objects even when assignment throws. Destructors must not throw for native tail-destruction equivalence; Rust Vec cleanup on destructor panic is not claimed as native exception parity.",
    "The private shiftLeft helper is implemented for the public Erase caller domain, where source end equals registered vector length; arbitrary protected helper subranges are not claimed.",
    "No C++ type-trait inference is introduced: nontrivial move assignment is supplied explicitly. Existing convenience APIs and trivial shifts remain separate policies.",
    "Native container aliases observing size during destructor callbacks are outside safe Rust exclusive borrowing; no metadata mirror or unsafe alias observer was introduced.",
    "Allocator hooks, ABI layout, zero-sized element equivalence, and writable const Array1 aliases remain outside this receipt.",
    "No global API/test ledger was changed; bounded rows and method bindings do not establish full class/kernel parity."
  ],
